@packages/compiler import { SigilAgentAdapter, SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator, SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess, SigilAgentCapabilityContract, SigilAgentEvaluationRequest }

component SigilPiCompilerAdapter {
  goal {
    Execute provider-neutral compiler evaluation through the Pi coding-agent CLI
    and return one validated result under an ephemeral, read-only tool boundary.
  }

  interface {
    PiAdapter {
      evaluate(SigilAgentEvaluationRequest) implements SigilAgentAdapter for provider
      pi with implementation identity builtin.pi-cli and the exact adapter package
      version

      SigilAgentCapabilityContract declares read-only workspace access, no agent-tool
      network, no approval escalation, and ephemeral state

      observability declares progress none, usage final, cost final, and
      post-settlement-only token and cost budget enforcement

      execution and cleanup failures use transport-neutral AdapterFailure kinds and
      preserve caller-visible recovery evidence
    }
  }
}

expand SigilPiCompilerAdapter {
  logic {
    PiAdapter {
      Validate the request and exact capability match before invocation. Send
      evaluationPrompt guidance through standard input to pi print mode with JSON
      events, selected workspace cwd, optional model, no session, no Pi skill,
      context, or extension discovery, no project-local approve, offline startup, and
      a hard tool allowlist of read, grep, find, ls, and bash.

      Supply Pi execution and cleanup hooks to SigilAgentAdapterExecutionCoordinator
      and use SigilAgentAdapterSubprocess for the private process lifecycle. Map
      process failure to execution and process-cleanup to cleanup while retaining
      transport-neutral caller recovery evidence.

      Parse the complete JSON event stream. Prefer assistant message_end text whose
      stopReason is stop or end_turn. If no such turn exists and exactly one
      assistant message_end has empty or missing stopReason, accept that text.
      Otherwise reject final-result-protocol. Require exactly one findings object in
      the selected terminal text. Map AdapterSubprocessFailure into AdapterFailure,
      retaining cleanup recovery evidence, before returning AgentEvaluationResult.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    PiExecutionBoundary {
      Enable bash so the evaluator can run the compiler inspection command policy
      families (sigil version, parse, check, fmt --check, glossary, graph, context,
      render, rg, sed, and git status/diff/show/log/grep/ls-files). Deny edit and
      write tools. Do not load Pi skills, AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md context files, or
      extensions for evaluation; compiler focus skills arrive only through
      evaluationPrompt.

      Use --no-session and never continue, resume, fork, or reuse a prior Pi session.
      Declare ephemeral state and never claim persistent execution.

      Accept only one terminal assistant result selected by stop or end_turn, or by
      the single empty-or-missing stopReason fallback; nonterminal tool-use turns are
      not terminal results.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    EphemeralPiExecution {
      Decision: Declare ephemeral state and invoke pi with --no-session so evaluation
      does not save a provider session.

      Scope: Governs Pi invocation and capability matching; excludes provider
      authentication and unrelated user Pi sessions outside this process.

      Trade-offs: Callers cannot resume an evaluation session through Pi session
      storage.

      Discarded alternatives: Persistent sessions like OpenCode were rejected because
      Pi documents an ephemeral CLI option.

      Consequences: Capability matching requests ephemeral state for Pi while fixed
      read-only, offline, and approval-free requirements remain.

      Revisit when: Pi removes or changes --no-session semantics.
    }

    ReadOnlyToolsWithBashForInspection {
      Decision: Allowlist the read, grep, find, ls, and bash tools and rely on the
      evaluator policy prompt to restrict shell commands to the read-only inspection
      families, including sigil inspection commands.

      Scope: Governs Pi --tools configuration and the trust boundary between the
      adapter and the compiler evaluation policy. Excludes per-command hard
      sandboxing at the adapter boundary beyond the tool allowlist.

      Trade-offs: Pi tool allowlists cannot enforce per-shell-command families, so
      bash compliance with the inspection policy depends on the model following the
      evaluator prompt.

      Discarded alternatives: Omitting bash was rejected because the compiler
      inspection policy advertises sigil, rg, sed, and git families that require a
      shell. Enabling edit or write tools was rejected as incompatible with read-only
      workspace access.

      Consequences: The adapter enables bash for allowed inspection commands. Edits,
      writes, Pi skill discovery, context files, and extensions remain disabled.

      Revisit when: Pi adds per-command shell allowlists or the evaluator policy's
      allowed command families change.
    }

    CompilerSkillViaPromptNotPiSkills {
      Decision: Pass --no-skills and embed compiler stage guidance only through
      evaluationPrompt so focus-selected evaluation skills remain authoritative.

      Scope: Governs Pi resource discovery flags for evaluation runs; excludes host
      skill installation destinations used by interactive Pi authoring.

      Trade-offs: Pi cannot auto-load compiler skill packages as Pi skills; the full
      guidance must fit the evaluation request transport.

      Design issues addressed: Focus selects compiler-native evaluation skills;
      loading Pi or project skills would compete with that authoritative guidance.

      Consequences: semantic-readiness, architecture-design, and other focus stages
      reach the model through the same provider-neutral prompt envelope used by Codex
      and OpenCode.
    }

    PiJsonEventTerminalResult {
      Decision: Select terminal assistant text from message_end stopReason stop or
      end_turn, with a fallback only when exactly one assistant message_end has empty
      or missing stopReason.

      Scope: Governs Pi JSON event parsing for the final findings object; excludes
      capability matching and tool allowlisting.

      Trade-offs: Intermediate tool-use narration cannot supply the findings object.

      Design issues addressed: Pi streams multiple assistant message_end events;
      treating every text blob as terminal would accept incomplete turns.

      Discarded alternatives: Always using the last assistant text regardless of
      stopReason was rejected because toolUse turns are nonterminal.

      Consequences: Missing stop turns fail final-result-protocol unless the single
      empty-reason fallback applies.

      Revisit when: Pi documents a different authoritative terminal event for print
      JSON mode.
    }
  }

  cases {
    PiAdapter {
      An ephemeral request invokes pi once and accepts one valid terminal result
      after process and output settlement.

      A persistent request mismatches before invocation.

      Bash remains available for allowed sigil inspection commands in the prompt
      policy; edit and write tools are not enabled.

      An assistant message_end with stopReason stop or end_turn supplies the terminal
      findings text.

      Multiple nonterminal toolUse assistant turns without a stop turn reject
      final-result-protocol.

      Timeout, cancellation, or process failure follows bounded subprocess cleanup.
    }
  }
}
